00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
THE SCALE OF ANDROID:
On the surface, Android is the free&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:08.560
open source operating system that runs the world.&nbsp;
Android have over 3 billion active users and over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.560 --> 00:00:13.800
a 1 billion Android phones are sold every single&nbsp;
year. But, unlike true open source software like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:20.840
Linux which is run by a non profit foundation,&nbsp;
Android is run by Google, and I’m sure you’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:26.560
figured that this leads to some ulterior motives.&nbsp;
By giving out Android for free, Google is able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:31.840
drive billions of users to all of their prominent&nbsp;
products whether that’s Gmail, Google Maps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.840 --> 00:00:36.920
Google Search, Google Assistant, Google Drive,&nbsp;
you name it. All of this obviously adds up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:42.800
quite a bit of auxiliary revenue but Google also&nbsp;
has much more direct methods of monetizing Android&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:49.440
as well. In fact, it turns out that Android isn’t&nbsp;
actually free at all. Since 2018, Google has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.520
charging phone makers up to $40 per device.&nbsp;
Technically, this fee isn’t for Android itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:00.920
but all of the Google apps that come along with&nbsp;
Android, but let’s be honest, Google apps and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:05.160
Android are pretty much synonymous at this&nbsp;
point. So phone makers are basically forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:10.960
to pay this licensing fee. And let’s not forget&nbsp;
about the crown jewel of Android: the Google Play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:16.600
Store. The Google Play store generates up to $48&nbsp;
billion in revenue per year over 2/3s of which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.600 --> 00:01:21.760
has historically been profit. Let me put that in&nbsp;
perspective. You know Windows? The big software&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:27.520
monopoly run by Microsoft that controls 69% of&nbsp;
the desktop market? Well, Windows as a whole only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:34.400
generated $24.7 billion throughout all of 2022. In&nbsp;
others words, Android generates twice the revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:39.800
of Windows using just the Google Play Store&nbsp;
alone. With that being said, I should mention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.120
that Android also costs Google a lot of money to&nbsp;
maintain. In fact, according to Google’s lawyers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:50.240
Google has spent over $40 billion to support&nbsp;
Android over the years. But, with more revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:55.680
than that coming in from just the Play Store every&nbsp;
single year, I suspect that Google is doing just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:08.420
fine. So, here’s the insane economics of Android&nbsp;
and how much Android is really worth to Google.

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.440
DIRECT MONETIZATION:
Starting from the sale of an Android phones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.880
we have the fee that Google charges Android&nbsp;
phone makers. While this fee can be as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.240
as $40 per device, the good news is that not&nbsp;
everyone actually pays this much. If you opt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.680
to include minimal Google services on your version&nbsp;
of Android, you can get away with paying as little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:30.240
as $2.50 per device. But according to Google&nbsp;
themselves, the majority of phonemakers will end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:35.960
up paying around $20 per device. At this point,&nbsp;
phone makers have been selling over a billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:40.800
Android phones every single year for nearly a&nbsp;
decade. So, I think it’s safe to estimate that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:45.960
Android phones sales alone are pulling in atleast&nbsp;
$20 billion for Google annually, and that’s before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:52.240
the real monetization even begins. Next up, we&nbsp;
have the Google Play Store. The Apple App Store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.240 --> 00:02:57.000
tends to garner a lot more media headlines because&nbsp;
they do generate a lot more revenue than the Play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:03.080
Store. In 2021, they generated $85 billion or&nbsp;
nearly twice the revenue of the Play Store despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:08.320
having less than have the users. But, just because&nbsp;
the Apple App Store is more lucrative and takes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:13.960
all the media heat doesn’t mean that the Play&nbsp;
Store is not lucrative. Google likes to claim that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:19.280
they have a super developer friendly pricing model&nbsp;
where 97% of developers don’t even have to pay a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:25.640
fee. But, that’s just because 97% of Play Store&nbsp;
developers don’t make any sort substantial revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.840
or they don’t use Google’s payment infrastructure.&nbsp;
When it comes to apps that are actually performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:34.960
well and rely on Google’s payment infrastructure&nbsp;
for revenue whether that be paid apps or in app&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:40.280
purchases, the Play Store pretty much has the&nbsp;
exact same pricing model as the Apple App Store.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:46.320
If your app makes less than $1 million per year,&nbsp;
Apple charges a tax of 15%. And if your app makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:53.000
more than $1 million per year, they charge a tax&nbsp;
of 30%. Now, you wanna guess what Google charges?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:59.160
Well, if your app makes less than $1 million per&nbsp;
year, Google charges 15%, and if your app makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:05.640
more than $1 million per year, Google charges 30%.&nbsp;
Google also charges 15% on subscription products&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:11.880
like streaming services and up to 15% on all other&nbsp;
transactions. So, despite being the free open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:18.040
source OS, Android has the same pricing as the&nbsp;
Apple’s closed private OS. But since iPhone users&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:22.840
are more likely to spend money on apps, Apple&nbsp;
takes all the heat for this pricing model. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.840 --> 00:04:28.800
meantime, Android is quietly racking in as much&nbsp;
as $48 billion per year. A couple of years ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.560
in a lawsuit between Oracle and Google, Oracle’s&nbsp;
lawyers estimated that out of the $31 billion in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:40.520
revenue the Play Store was making at the time,&nbsp;
$22 billion was profit aka 70%. Even if this has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:45.760
dropped closer to 66%, we’re looking at $32&nbsp;
billion in profit from just the Play Store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:52.480
alone. For perspective, Google as a whole only&nbsp;
makes $78 billion in profit per year. So, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:58.760
Play Store alone might account for as much as 41%&nbsp;
of Google’s entire bottom line. When you combine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:03.960
this with licensing fees from phone makers, we’re&nbsp;
left with a total of $68 billion worth of direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:10.080
revenue from Android or a little over a fifth&nbsp;
of Google’s entire revenue. And all of that is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:23.338
just from direct monetization. Google’s indirect&nbsp;
monetization efforts are far more interesting.

00:05:23.338 --> 00:05:25.440
INDIRECT MONETIZATION:
Given that Google Apps comes preinstalled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:31.680
on Android devices, billions of users are driven&nbsp;
towards these apps every single year, but I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:37.040
think you quite realize just how lucrative this&nbsp;
is. Personally, I always assumed that the split&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:42.800
between mobile revenue and desktop revenue was&nbsp;
more or less equal, but it turns out that mobile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.840
is actually far more profitable across the board&nbsp;
especially when it comes to certain apps like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:54.080
Google Maps. Morgan Stanley estimates that Google&nbsp;
Maps revenue will total $11 billion in 2023 out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:06:01.560
of which $9.82 billion or 90% is thanks to mobile&nbsp;
devices. For obvious reasons, Maps is more skewed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:08.560
towards mobile devices than other Google apps but&nbsp;
trend holds across the board. In fact, over 70% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.560 --> 00:06:14.520
YouTube watch time comes from mobile devices and&nbsp;
63% of Google’s search traffic comes from mobile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:20.080
devices. It’s not just users who prefer mobile&nbsp;
either. It turns out that advertisers also like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:25.840
to spend a lot more money on mobile ads as 60%&nbsp;
of global digital ad spends comes form mobile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.840 --> 00:06:31.200
devices. So, there’s no question that controlling&nbsp;
a mobile OS is lucrative, even more lucrative than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:38.400
controlling a desktop OS, but it’s a lot harder&nbsp;
to quantify this. Thankfully, Google has put on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.280
number on this with their partnership with Apple.&nbsp;
You know how Safari and Siri and other Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:48.200
services direct you to Google search? Well, this&nbsp;
isn’t out of the kindness of their heart or some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.200 --> 00:06:53.520
sort of affinity for Google. The reality is that&nbsp;
Google actually pays Apple a boatload of money for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.520 --> 00:07:00.080
Google to be the primary search engine on Apple&nbsp;
devices. $18 billion per year to be precise. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:05.040
that’s just for Google search to be the default&nbsp;
search engine. It has nothing to do with Maps or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:10.520
Photos or Drive or Gmail or Chrome or any of the&nbsp;
other Google services that come preinstalled on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:15.510
Android devices. Some of these services, people&nbsp;
would likely use regardless of whether it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.510 --> 00:07:20.640
preinstalled or not. For example, people aren’t&nbsp;
gonna switch from Gmail to Outlook just because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:26.280
the Gmail app does or does not come pre installed.&nbsp;
Similarly, people aren’t gonna switch from YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:31.880
to DailyMotion just because Android doesn’t come&nbsp;
with YouTube. So, for certain apps, the benefit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.880 --> 00:07:37.640
of controlling Android is not that large, but for&nbsp;
other apps, it makes all the difference like Maps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:42.960
for example. When Apple first launched their&nbsp;
own version of Maps in 2012, the vast majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:48.720
of iPhone users were extremely disappointed. But&nbsp;
by simply continuing to put it in front of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:53.760
and improving the platform, Apple was able to&nbsp;
increase Apple maps usage to be 3 times higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:08:00.000
than Google maps usage on iOS devices by the end&nbsp;
of 2015 itself. So by this point, it’s likely that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:07.120
80-90% of iPhone users just use Apple maps. So,&nbsp;
Google’s ability to force Google Maps onto Android&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:13.480
users is extremely valuable. Out of the $9.82&nbsp;
billion that Google generates from mobile devices,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:19.400
likely $7 to $8 billion is directly thanks to&nbsp;
Android. Another instance in which the OS makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:25.360
a big difference is cloud storage. Apple hasn’t&nbsp;
released iCloud stats in a while but as of 2018,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:31.480
they had 850 million iCloud users out of which&nbsp;
170 million were paying. By now, that number is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:38.160
likely closer to 250 or 300 million paying users.&nbsp;
Each paying user only generates about $2 to $3 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.160 --> 00:08:45.360
revenue per month, but at that scale, it adds&nbsp;
up to roughly $8 billion. Now, iCloud is great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.520
and all but it’s likely that most of these users&nbsp;
just use iCloud because it’s already there, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:56.680
part of the Apple ecosystem, and it works. It’s&nbsp;
likely the same case with Google Photos and Drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.680 --> 00:09:02.280
as well. A lot of people who use and pay for those&nbsp;
services likely do it just because it’s already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:08.480
there, it’s part of the Google ecosystem, and&nbsp;
it works. Combine this with the $18 billion that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:13.520
Google thinks mobile search is worth and the $7 to&nbsp;
$8 billion from Maps and we’re already up to $34&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:20.000
billion from indirect monetization. We can throw&nbsp;
on more Google services on top of this like Chrome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:25.200
and Google Assistant and Google Docs and Google&nbsp;
Meet and Google Calendar and we’d probably end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.760
with something like $50 billion. Combine this with&nbsp;
the $68 billion from their direct monetization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.760 --> 00:09:36.160
efforts and we’re left with $118 billion in&nbsp;
revenue. To be conservative, we can even call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:47.894
it $100 billion which brings us into the question&nbsp;
of the day: how much is Android worth to Google?

00:09:47.894 --> 00:09:49.680
THE TRUE VALUE OF ANDROID:
Well, keeping things simple, Google&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.680 --> 00:09:56.240
pulls in just under $300 billion worth of revenue&nbsp;
every year and they’re worth $1.67 trillion. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:01.000
given that Android itself conservatively accounts&nbsp;
for $100 billion of this, you could say that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:09.520
Android is worth $550 billion. That would nearly&nbsp;
make Android a top 10 company by itself. No wonder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.520 --> 00:10:15.120
Google wanted Android’s founder, Andy Rubin, to be&nbsp;
the next CEO of Google until he got caught up in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:21.960
scandal. Android is obviously not the open source&nbsp;
free software that Google likes to position it as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.960 --> 00:10:27.040
but honest, putting the insane economics of&nbsp;
Android aside, the true value of Android is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:32.720
much more than just financial gain. Android&nbsp;
gives Google power over the entire smartphone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:39.280
experience of over 3 billion people. This gives&nbsp;
them unprecedented access into the innerworkings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:44.320
of every aspect of these people’s online and&nbsp;
personal lives. What are their interests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:48.800
how much money do they make, how does their family&nbsp;
life look like, where do they spend their money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.800 --> 00:10:53.400
how do their political beliefs look like, and so&nbsp;
much more that I can never truly list out because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.400 --> 00:10:58.280
Google takes full advantage of this. Think about&nbsp;
this. Each Android device sends Google 1 MB of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.280 --> 00:11:04.520
data every 12 hours even when left idle. For&nbsp;
perspective, each iOS devices only sends Apple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.520 --> 00:11:11.880
52 KB or 1/20th of the data every 12 hours. This&nbsp;
means that Android collects 2 MB of data every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:18.640
day from every Android user even if they’re not&nbsp;
actively using their phones. From the entire 3.3&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:25.680
billion Android user base, that translates to 6.6&nbsp;
Petabytes of data every single day or 2.4 Exabytes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:31.560
of data every single year. Google likes to assert&nbsp;
that this does not accurately depict their data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:36.480
collection efforts because a lot of this data is&nbsp;
just software updates and device status related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:42.720
things. But just because a lot of it is harmless&nbsp;
doesn’t mean that all of it is, and considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.720 --> 00:11:47.240
that Google’s entire business model is collecting&nbsp;
data and showing ads, it’s likely that a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:51.960
amount of is more than what you’d be comfortable&nbsp;
sharing. Things like the location data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:58.600
search history, phone usage, online purchases, and&nbsp;
even biometrics of 3.3 billion people, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:06.680
is the real power of Android. The $100 billion in&nbsp;
revenue from Android is really just a bonus. With&nbsp;&nbsp;

